<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<component type="desktop-application">
  <id>dev.cominotti.lushtext</id>

  <name>LushText</name>
  <summary>A minimalist text editor with workspace support</summary>

  <developer id="dev.cominotti">
    <name>Danilo Cominotti</name>
  </developer>

  <metadata_license>CC0-1.0</metadata_license>
  <project_license>GPL-3.0-or-later</project_license>

  <description>
    <p>
      LushText is a fast, minimalist text editor for GNOME. It combines a
      focused GtkSourceView editing surface with persistent workspaces,
      adaptive document properties, Markdown preview, notes, local history,
      and careful draft recovery.
    </p>
    <p>Features:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Persistent workspace sidebar with ordered workspace folders</li>
      <li>Adaptive document properties pane for metadata, file health, and formatting details</li>
      <li>Focus Mode for distraction-free writing</li>
      <li>Native Markdown preview with tables, task lists, callouts, footnotes, and local images</li>
      <li>Syntax highlighting via GtkSourceView</li>
      <li>EditorConfig-aware indentation settings</li>
      <li>Bookmarks, document notes, and folder notes</li>
      <li>Local history snapshots for saved files</li>
      <li>Workspace search and safe multi-file Replace All with undo</li>
      <li>Session restore, autosaved drafts, and external file change monitoring</li>
      <li>Large-file safeguards, minimap, dark mode, and customizable editor font</li>
    </ul>
  </description>

  <launchable type="desktop-id">dev.cominotti.lushtext.desktop</launchable>

  <url type="homepage">https://github.com/cominotti/lushtext</url>
  <url type="bugtracker">https://github.com/cominotti/lushtext/issues</url>
  <url type="vcs-browser">https://github.com/cominotti/lushtext</url>

  <branding>
    <color type="primary" scheme_preference="light">#d946ef</color>
    <color type="primary" scheme_preference="dark">#8b5cf6</color>
  </branding>

  <content_rating type="oars-1.1"/>

  <supports>
    <control>pointing</control>
    <control>keyboard</control>
  </supports>

  <requires>
    <display_length compare="ge">360</display_length>
  </requires>

  <screenshots>
    <screenshot type="default">
      <caption>Editing a text file with the workspace sidebar</caption>
      <image>https://raw.githubusercontent.com/cominotti/lushtext/main/data/screenshots/editor.png</image>
    </screenshot>
  </screenshots>

  <releases>
    <release version="0.8.3" date="2026-09-22">
      <description>
        <p>## Poetic Opening</p>
        <p>Source checked: Edgar Allan Poe, "Eldorado" (1849), as printed in The Complete Poems of Edgar Allan Poe; four six-line stanzas, this is the first.</p>
        <p>Selection: Complete stanza.</p>
        <p>Complete stanza:</p>
        <p>&gt; Gaily bedight,</p>
        <p>&gt; A gallant knight,</p>
        <p>&gt; In sunshine and in shadow,</p>
        <p>&gt; Had journeyed long,</p>
        <p>&gt; Singing a song,</p>
        <p>&gt; In search of Eldorado.</p>
        <p>Edgar Allan Poe, "Eldorado"</p>
        <p>## What's Changed</p>
        <p>This is a small patch release. It makes the Markdown preview keep up with the document you are actually looking at.</p>
        <p>- **The preview follows you into new files.** If you are reading in preview-only mode (Alt+P) or with the side-by-side preview open, opening another Markdown file now shows its rendered content right away. You stay in the preview mode you were in.</p>
        <p>- **A clear "Preparing Markdown preview…" message** now appears while a Markdown file is still loading. Before, you briefly saw an empty page.</p>
        <p>- **The preview also refreshes after other whole-document updates.** This covers reloading a file that changed on disk, restoring a draft or a local-history snapshot, and saving with formatting applied.</p>
        <p>- **Save As and rename update the preview too.** Saving an untitled document as `notes.md` renders it as Markdown. Renaming a `.md` file to `.txt` shows "Not a Markdown file".</p>
        <p>## Manual Actions Needed</p>
        <p>No manual action is needed for this release. Update through your usual Flatpak remote.</p>
        <p>## Warnings and Deprecations</p>
        <p>There are no warnings or deprecations in this release. Creating a new untitled document still leaves preview-only mode, as before, so you can start typing straight away.</p>
        <p>## Bug Fixes</p>
        <p>- Opening a Markdown file while the preview was active no longer shows an empty preview until you toggle the preview off and on again.</p>
        <p>- Renaming a Markdown file to an extension with no known language no longer keeps its Markdown syntax highlighting and preview.</p>
        <p>- Opening the side-by-side preview right after the first document appears no longer prints a GTK size-allocation warning.</p>
        <p>- A folder you expanded in the workspace sidebar no longer collapses again by itself when a background check of whether that folder is empty finishes late.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.8.2" date="2026-09-21">
      <description>
        <p>## Poetic Opening</p>
        <p>Source checked: the 1609 Quarto text as transcribed on Wikisource and in the Folger Shakespeare edition, which agree on the wording and on the sonnet's three quatrains plus couplet. The quatrain below is the complete first quatrain, closing its own ABAB rhyme on behold, hang, cold, sang.</p>
        <p>Selection: Complete stanza.</p>
        <p>Complete stanza:</p>
        <p>&gt; That time of year thou mayst in me behold</p>
        <p>&gt; When yellow leaves, or none, or few, do hang</p>
        <p>&gt; Upon those boughs which shake against the cold,</p>
        <p>&gt; Bare ruined choirs, where late the sweet birds sang.</p>
        <p>William Shakespeare, "Sonnet 73"</p>
        <p>A release about boughs that should not shake: the rows of a tree, when you click among them.</p>
        <p>## What's Changed</p>
        <p>This is a focused patch release with one user-facing fix, described under Bug Fixes. Behind it, the way the sidebar's file tree is hosted was corrected at its root rather than patched, and the tests that guard it now measure the screen rather than the numbers:</p>
        <p>- **The tree's geometry is now handed to it in the frame it actually uses.** The file list has a small amount of padding from the GNOME sidebar style, and it measures itself inside that padding. LushText was handing it measurements that ignored the padding, so the list corrected them on every layout pass and, while doing so, re-derived its scroll position a pixel or two away from where it had been placed. That correction is gone at the source.</p>
        <p>- **Tests now check where rows are drawn.** Every earlier test of this area read the scroll position and accepted a row anywhere inside the visible area with a pixel of slack. New tests pin a named row's exact position across a sequence of layout passes while other rows are selected and focused, in the sidebar and in the reusable widget's own suite, so a shift of one pixel fails loudly instead of trembling on screen.</p>
        <p>## Manual Actions Needed</p>
        <p>No manual action is needed for this release. There is no data migration, no change to any file LushText stores, no setting to reset, and no cache to clear. Update and carry on.</p>
        <p>## Warnings and Deprecations</p>
        <p>Nothing is removed or deprecated. One behaviour is worth stating plainly because it is unchanged on purpose: when you move keyboard focus to a row that is partly cut off at the bottom of the sidebar, the sidebar scrolls to reveal it, and if the workspace header is above the list it scrolls that header out of view to do so. A gentler scroll that moved only by the cut-off amount was built and tested, and it lost keyboard requests made in the frame between the scroll and the relayout, so it was not shipped. Scrolling back up brings the header back, as it has since v0.7.1.</p>
        <p>## Bug Fixes</p>
        <p>- **Clicking or focusing rows in the sidebar no longer shifts the file tree by a few pixels.** With the sidebar scrolled to the top, moving between rows would sometimes nudge the whole tree one to three pixels up or down; sometimes it stayed there, sometimes it snapped back on the next click. The list was re-deriving its scroll position against measurements that disagreed with its own by exactly the padding of the sidebar style, and rendering against the result. It is now handed measurements it has no reason to correct, and any residual disagreement is written back within the same layout pass, so rows are drawn where they were placed.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.8.1" date="2026-09-18">
      <description>
        <p>## Poetic Opening</p>
        <p>Source checked: the 1609 Quarto text as transcribed on Wikisource and in the Folger Shakespeare edition, which agree on the wording and on the sonnet's three quatrains plus couplet. The quatrain below is the complete third quatrain, closing its own ABAB rhyme on despising, state, arising, gate.</p>
        <p>Selection: Complete stanza.</p>
        <p>Complete stanza:</p>
        <p>&gt; Yet in these thoughts myself almost despising,</p>
        <p>&gt; Haply I think on thee, and then my state,</p>
        <p>&gt; Like to the lark at break of day arising</p>
        <p>&gt; From sullen earth, sings hymns at heaven's gate;</p>
        <p>William Shakespeare, "Sonnet 29"</p>
        <p>A small release about arising: scrolling back up, and finding the top where you left it.</p>
        <p>## What's Changed</p>
        <p>This is a focused patch release. Its one user-facing change is a fix, described under Bug Fixes, and the rest of the work happened behind the scenes:</p>
        <p>- **The visual proof lane that guards LushText's rendering now measures animations correctly.** Two timing defects in the tool that records real sidebar animations frame by frame made it report failures that were not real: it triggered the animation before the screen recorder had produced its first frame, and it stopped checking geometry before the recorder stopped capturing. Both are fixed, and the lane was run repeatedly to confirm it stays green rather than passing once by luck. This does not change anything you see in the app, but it is what let this fix be verified to the standard the project holds itself to.</p>
        <p>- **Two GitHub release workflows no longer race to create the same release.** In v0.8.0 they did, and the duplicate had to be removed by hand before the authored notes could be published. Creation now belongs to one workflow and the other waits.</p>
        <p>- **Release tooling installs only prebuilt Python packages**, closing a supply-chain finding in the release lane's dependency installation.</p>
        <p>## Manual Actions Needed</p>
        <p>No manual action is needed for this release. There is no data migration, no change to any file LushText stores, no setting to reset, and no cache to clear. Update and carry on.</p>
        <p>## Warnings and Deprecations</p>
        <p>Nothing is removed or deprecated. One note for the record:</p>
        <p>- **Flathub publication is not planned.** This was decided during the v0.8.0 cycle and is now written into the project specifications. The Cominotti Flatpak repository at flatpak.cominotti.dev remains the one supported channel, and nothing changes for anyone already installed from it.</p>
        <p>## Bug Fixes</p>
        <p>- **Scrolling the workspace sidebar back to the top shows the workspace header again.** After scrolling down with the mouse wheel or touchpad, scrolling back up would stop about 55 pixels short, leaving the workspace name and its buttons hidden just above the visible area, and it would refuse to go further. It did not happen every time, and switching to another window and back would often clear it, which made it feel random. The cause was not random: the file list settles its own geometry by a few pixels during each layout pass, and the sidebar was misreading that settle as a request to scroll, one or two pixels at a time, until the header was gone. The sidebar now recognises a settle for what it is and only follows the list when it genuinely asks to move, such as when keyboard focus lands on a row out of view.</p>
        <p>- This is the same visible symptom v0.7.1 repaired, reached by a different route. That earlier fix is intact; this one closes the second route, and the tests now drive the sidebar with wheel-sized steps instead of a single jump, so this path is covered as well.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.8.0" date="2026-09-16">
      <description>
        <p>## Poetic Opening</p>
        <p>Source checked: Portuguese Wikisource and escritas.org transcriptions of the sonnet, which agree on the wording and on the Petrarchan structure of two quatrains followed by two tercets, rhyming ABBA ABBA CDC EDE. The quatrain below is complete and closes its own ABBA rhyme on maior, beija, seja, Dor.</p>
        <p>Selection: Complete original first quatrain and complete English quatrain.</p>
        <p>Original (complete stanza):</p>
        <p>&gt; Ser poeta é ser mais alto, é ser maior</p>
        <p>&gt; Do que os homens! Morder como quem beija!</p>
        <p>&gt; É ser mendigo e dar como quem seja</p>
        <p>&gt; Rei do Reino de Aquém e de Além Dor!</p>
        <p>English (complete stanza, working translation):</p>
        <p>&gt; To be a poet is to stand higher, to be greater</p>
        <p>&gt; than other men! To bite as one who kisses!</p>
        <p>&gt; To be a beggar and to give as one who is</p>
        <p>&gt; King of the Realm of This Side and Beyond Pain!</p>
        <p>Florbela Espanca, "Ser Poeta" ("To Be a Poet")</p>
        <p>To stand higher is to see further than your own window, which is what this release is about.</p>
        <p>## What's Changed</p>
        <p>**LushText now notices what changed while you were away.**</p>
        <p>Until now the command palette kept a snapshot of your workspace and had no way to learn that the world had moved. Switch a git branch, run a build, or create a file from a terminal, and `Ctrl+Shift+P` simply did not know the file existed — not after a second, not after an hour. The only ways to correct it were changing workspace scope, toggling hidden files, or restarting the app.</p>
        <p>- **The command palette refreshes when your attention returns.** Coming back to the window, or opening the palette, brings the file list up to date. Files that appeared are searchable; files that were deleted stop being offered.</p>
        <p>- **Collapsed folders stop claiming to be empty.** A folder marked `(Empty)` with no expander arrow and a greyed-out `Focus Folder` was reporting what its parent saw the last time it looked. If something was created inside it since, returning to the window corrects the label, the arrow, and the action — and the reverse, for a folder emptied on disk.</p>
        <p>- **Saving a new file makes it searchable at once.** Saving an untitled document, or using Save As, previously produced a file that LushText itself could not find in its own palette. It is indexed the moment it is written, with no waiting.</p>
        <p>**The refresh tries hard not to be in your way.**</p>
        <p>- It is silent. No status message, no screen-reader announcement, and your selection, keyboard focus, scroll position, and which folders are open are all preserved.</p>
        <p>- It is throttled, and the throttle adapts. The gap before the next refresh is derived from how long the last one actually took, so a small project stays snappy while a very large or network-mounted workspace slows itself down on its own rather than hammering the disk.</p>
        <p>- It yields to your work. While a save, a window close, a draft autosave, or a file chooser is in flight, a refresh is refused outright rather than queued in front of the operation that is protecting your bytes.</p>
        <p>- It shares. Several windows open on the same workspace refresh it once between them, not once each.</p>
        <p>Verified against a real 87,624-file workspace: a file created from a terminal in a folder that had never been expanded became searchable 3.4 seconds after returning to the window.</p>
        <p>## Manual Actions Needed</p>
        <p>No manual action is needed for this release. There is no data migration, no change to any file LushText stores, no setting to reset, and no cache to clear. Update and carry on.</p>
        <p>## Warnings and Deprecations</p>
        <p>Nothing is removed or deprecated. A few honest limits are worth knowing, because they are places you might reasonably expect the new behaviour and not get it:</p>
        <p>- **Refreshing happens when your attention returns, and at no other time.** There is deliberately no background timer. If you stay in the LushText window while a build runs elsewhere on screen, nothing refreshes until you leave and come back, or open the palette.</p>
        <p>- **The sidebar tree updates on window focus, not on palette open.** Opening the palette refreshes only the file list, because the palette does not read the tree and re-scanning it would be work for nobody.</p>
        <p>- **A refused moment is not saved for later.** If you return while a save or autosave is running, or return twice in quick succession, that moment does nothing and is not replayed. The next return starts the refresh.</p>
        <p>- **A slow workspace will let itself fall behind.** The adaptive interval can stretch to a minute on a very large tree or a network mount. That is the trade being made on purpose: freshness lags rather than the disk being pounded.</p>
        <p>- **Expect a little more disk activity than before.** Refreshing re-reads the workspace. On an ordinary project this is unnoticeable; on a workspace folder pointed at something enormous, such as your whole home directory, it is real work happening more often than it used to.</p>
        <p>- **Hidden and excluded entries stay invisible.** `Show Hidden Files` and `Always Excluded Names` still apply, so external changes inside `.git` — or inside build directories the palette skips, such as `node_modules` and `target` — remain out of sight by design.</p>
        <p>- **The folder-state correction is not announced.** If a screen reader user is focused on a collapsed folder row when its state is corrected, the expander and `Focus Folder` change without a spoken notification. Interrupting someone for a change they did not ask for was judged worse than the stale affordance it replaces.</p>
        <p>- **Files outside every configured workspace folder are never indexed**, including a Save As to a location outside your workspace. That is unchanged behaviour, restated because the new freshness might suggest otherwise.</p>
        <p>## Bug Fixes</p>
        <p>- Files created, renamed, or removed outside LushText no longer stay invisible to the command palette until a workspace change or a restart.</p>
        <p>- The palette no longer offers you the working tree from before your last branch switch.</p>
        <p>- A collapsed folder that has gained content no longer keeps its empty label, its missing expander arrow, and its disabled `Focus Folder` action.</p>
        <p>- Saving an untitled document, or saving under a new name, no longer creates a file that LushText cannot find in its own search.</p>
        <p>- Deleted files stop appearing as palette results instead of lingering until the next restart.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.7.1" date="2026-09-15">
      <description>
        <p>## Poetic Opening</p>
        <p>Source checked: Wikisource, "Sensation" from Rimbaud's *Poésies* (written March 1870), complete first quatrain of two.</p>
        <p>Selection: Complete original quatrain and complete English quatrain.</p>
        <p>Original (complete stanza):</p>
        <p>&gt; Par les soirs bleus d'été, j'irai dans les sentiers,</p>
        <p>&gt; Picoté par les blés, fouler l'herbe menue :</p>
        <p>&gt; Rêveur, j'en sentirai la fraîcheur à mes pieds.</p>
        <p>&gt; Je laisserai le vent baigner ma tête nue.</p>
        <p>English (complete stanza, working translation):</p>
        <p>&gt; On blue summer evenings I will walk the footpaths,</p>
        <p>&gt; pricked by the wheat, treading the fine grass:</p>
        <p>&gt; dreaming, I will feel its coolness at my feet.</p>
        <p>&gt; I will let the wind bathe my bare head.</p>
        <p>Arthur Rimbaud, "Sensation"</p>
        <p>## What's Changed</p>
        <p>This is a small, focused patch. It repairs a workspace sidebar regression that</p>
        <p>v0.7.0 introduced, and it does nothing else.</p>
        <p>- **Your workspace bar is back.** The sidebar row carrying the workspace name,</p>
        <p>  the collapse arrow, and the add-folder and refresh buttons stays where it</p>
        <p>  belongs, at the top of the workspace list.</p>
        <p>- **The sidebar scrolls where you send it.** It no longer pulls itself back</p>
        <p>  down a moment after you scroll up, and with more than one workspace it no</p>
        <p>  longer jitters between two positions.</p>
        <p>- **Scrolling near the workspace bar feels right.** The file tree now treats</p>
        <p>  the strip behind that bar as off screen, which is what it always should have</p>
        <p>  done. Revealing a row with the keyboard, or jumping to a file, lands it where</p>
        <p>  you can actually see it rather than just under the bar.</p>
        <p>Nothing else in the editor changed. No new settings, no new shortcuts, no</p>
        <p>changes to how your files, drafts, sessions, notes, or workspaces are stored.</p>
        <p>## Manual Actions Needed</p>
        <p>No manual action is needed for this release. Your workspaces, open tabs,</p>
        <p>settings, notes, and history carry over untouched. If v0.7.0 left your sidebar</p>
        <p>scrolled oddly, simply opening v0.7.1 puts it right.</p>
        <p>## Warnings and Deprecations</p>
        <p>No deprecations, and nothing is being removed. This release adds no new</p>
        <p>warnings; it removes a defect that had been quietly hiding a second one.</p>
        <p>## Bug Fixes</p>
        <p>- **The workspace bar could be scrolled out of reach.** In v0.7.0 the sidebar</p>
        <p>  scrolled itself down by the exact height of the workspace bar as soon as a</p>
        <p>  workspace was shown, leaving that bar hidden above the visible area. Trying</p>
        <p>  to scroll back up snapped the list straight to the tree's first row. The</p>
        <p>  cause was inside the container that keeps the file tree fast on very large</p>
        <p>  folders: it mistook its own resting position for a request from the tree to</p>
        <p>  scroll somewhere else, and then honoured that request on every layout pass.</p>
        <p>- **Two or more workspaces made the sidebar oscillate.** The same mistake made</p>
        <p>  each workspace section pull the sidebar in the opposite direction from its</p>
        <p>  neighbour, so the view bounced between two positions indefinitely and the end</p>
        <p>  of the list could never be reached. Both symptoms share one fix.</p>
        <p>- **Revealed rows could land behind the workspace bar.** Correcting the above</p>
        <p>  exposed a second defect it had been masking: the file tree was told it had</p>
        <p>  more visible space than it really did, by the height of the bar above it, so</p>
        <p>  a row revealed by keyboard navigation or by jumping to a file could come to</p>
        <p>  rest just under that bar. The tree is now given the space it genuinely has.</p>
        <p>This fix lives in the reusable GTK Lush widget layer rather than in LushText's</p>
        <p>own sidebar code, so anything else built on that container gets the same</p>
        <p>repair. The behaviour is now covered at four levels, from pure unit and</p>
        <p>property tests through widget-level and full sidebar journeys, with each new</p>
        <p>check verified to fail against the original defect.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.7.0" date="2026-09-15">
      <description>
        <p>## Poetic Opening</p>
        <p>Source checked: Wikisource, "Correspondances" from Les Fleurs du mal (1861 edition), complete first quatrain.</p>
        <p>Selection: Complete original quatrain and complete English quatrain.</p>
        <p>Original (complete stanza):</p>
        <p>&gt; La Nature est un temple où de vivants piliers</p>
        <p>&gt; Laissent parfois sortir de confuses paroles;</p>
        <p>&gt; L'homme y passe à travers des forêts de symboles</p>
        <p>&gt; Qui l'observent avec des regards familiers.</p>
        <p>English (complete stanza, working translation):</p>
        <p>&gt; Nature is a temple where living pillars</p>
        <p>&gt; Sometimes let slip confused words;</p>
        <p>&gt; There man passes through forests of symbols</p>
        <p>&gt; That watch him with familiar eyes.</p>
        <p>Charles Baudelaire, "Correspondances"</p>
        <p>## What's Changed</p>
        <p>This release is about seeing your files clearly. LushText now lets you decide what stays hidden, draws large folders completely instead of stopping partway, and finishes rendering long Markdown documents that it used to cut short. Alongside that, a long run of internal restructuring uncovered and fixed a group of real data-safety defects, including one that could destroy a file without warning.</p>
        <p>You can now show hidden files across your whole workspace with a single switch. Turning it on reveals dotfiles everywhere at once: in the sidebar tree, in the command palette file index, and in workspace content search. Reach it however suits you, with Ctrl+Shift+H, the new Show Hidden Files item in the main menu, or the new Hidden and Excluded Items group in Preferences under Workspace. The switch is off by default, so LushText behaves exactly as it did before until you ask for more.</p>
        <p>Some names should stay out of the way even when hidden files are shown, so this release adds an editable Always Excluded Names list. Names on that list are never listed, never indexed, and never searched. It ships with .git, you can add and remove entries one row at a time, and a reset button restores the default. Folders you have deliberately added to a workspace always remain visible, no matter what the list says.</p>
        <p>Workspace search gained a per-search Hidden toggle beside the existing case, regular expression, whole word, and gitignore controls. It starts from your global setting each time the panel opens, so you can look inside hidden files once without changing how the rest of the app behaves. The toggle is remembered with your saved searches and shown in the search history summary.</p>
        <p>The workspace sidebar now draws large folders completely. Previously a folder with more than roughly two hundred entries rendered its first rows and then left blank space below, even though every entry was really there. The file tree is now properly virtualized, so long directories scroll smoothly and show every file.</p>
        <p>Markdown preview no longer gives up at the first oversized block. A single very large table, list, code block, or quotation used to discard the entire rest of the document. Large blocks now render completely across multiple passes, and when one indivisible piece genuinely cannot fit, only that piece is replaced by a marker in place while everything around it still renders. The preview tells you when it has simplified something and how many pieces were affected. The whole-document limits still apply and still say so plainly.</p>
        <p>Workspace search now finishes honestly when it hits its ten thousand match ceiling. It previously dropped the last buffered matches and never mentioned the truncation; it now delivers them and tells you the result set was cut short so you can narrow the query.</p>
        <p>## Manual Actions Needed</p>
        <p>No manual action is needed after upgrading. Both new settings default to LushText's previous behavior, so nothing is reconfigured, reset, or rebuilt for you.</p>
        <p>No on-disk format changed in this release. Your workspaces, sessions, drafts, notes, bookmarks, local history, search history, and saved searches all load unchanged.</p>
        <p>Downstream packagers can build from the release commit as usual. The Rust dependency set is unchanged since 0.6.0, so the vendored Cargo sources used for offline Flatpak builds are still valid.</p>
        <p>## Warnings and Deprecations</p>
        <p>Nothing is deprecated and no feature was removed in this release.</p>
        <p>Saved searches and search history now record whether hidden files were included. Older files load fine in this version, but if you downgrade to 0.6.0 after saving searches here, that behavior has not been tested; keeping a copy of those files before downgrading is the cautious choice.</p>
        <p>One known limitation is worth stating plainly, because it is easy to run into and is not fixed here. If you rename or move a file outside LushText, using mv, git mv, or a file manager, the notes and bookmarks attached to that file are left behind and will not follow it. Renaming from inside the LushText sidebar does migrate them correctly. This is tracked for a future release.</p>
        <p>Search result ordering and the sidebar's scrolling behavior both changed internally this cycle. Results and rows should be correct and complete, but they may not appear in exactly the same order or scroll position as in 0.6.0.</p>
        <p>## Bug Fixes</p>
        <p>Renaming a file onto the name of a file that already exists no longer destroys the other file. This was reachable in a few keystrokes and was silent, permanent, and impossible to undo. LushText now refuses the rename and tells you that a file with that name already exists in the folder.</p>
        <p>Saving a document while renaming or deleting the same file in the sidebar no longer corrupts the result. The save could previously recreate the old filename with your text while the open tab quietly pointed at a stale file, and report success anyway.</p>
        <p>Creating a workspace while LushText was still starting up could overwrite every other stored workspace. Startup and new workspaces are now merged instead of racing.</p>
        <p>Cancelling a new file or a rename no longer risks deleting a different, real file that had been moved onto that path in the meantime, and deleting a broken symbolic link now actually deletes it instead of silently doing nothing.</p>
        <p>Folders you had expanded no longer collapse on their own when the sidebar refreshes, and renaming a clean file no longer disables that tab's fast first-keystroke autosave.</p>
        <p>Notes and bookmarks received a group of fixes. A single unresolvable note conflict used to abandon every note sorted after it; conflicts are now isolated. Two overlapping renames could strand a note permanently while reporting success. A rename still in progress during session restore could show a restored tab with no bookmarks, and your next bookmark change would then write that empty state back over the real one. A note that failed to save used to throw away the text you had just typed, and now reopens with your words intact. Bookmark saves now retry on failure and are flushed when LushText closes, and warn you if they could not be saved.</p>
        <p>Several editor and document fixes landed as well. A tab could get stuck permanently read-only with undo disabled after an interrupted file load. Cancelling the save changes dialog when closing a tab left that tab's autosave silently switched off, leaving unsaved work unprotected. A cancelled load could let the next autosave overwrite a good draft with nearly empty content. Session restore could stall partway through, and a save could begin against a document that had not finished loading. A failure inside session saving no longer cascades into every later save.</p>
        <p>The encoding, line ending, and file health dialogs had an accessibility gap that is now closed.</p>
        <p>Replace All is safer under interruption. Its rollback path now checks each target before writing rather than restoring unconditionally; this closes a hazard rather than a defect you were likely to have hit in practice.</p>
        <p>For contributors, the development helper that clears LushText's application data no longer deletes drafts, history, configuration, and cache when given any argument at all, including --help. It now asks for confirmation and understands --help, --dry-run, and --yes.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.6.0" date="2026-07-23">
      <description>
        <p>## Poetic Opening</p>
        <p>Source checked: Wikisource, "A Dream Within a Dream" (from The Works of the Late Edgar Allan Poe, 1859), complete second stanza.</p>
        <p>Selection: Complete printed stanza.</p>
        <p>Complete stanza:</p>
        <p>&gt; I stand amid the roar</p>
        <p>&gt; Of a surf-tormented shore,</p>
        <p>&gt; And I hold within my hand</p>
        <p>&gt; Grains of the golden sand—</p>
        <p>&gt; How few! yet how they creep</p>
        <p>&gt; Through my fingers to the deep,</p>
        <p>&gt; While I weep—while I weep!</p>
        <p>&gt; O God! can I not grasp</p>
        <p>&gt; Them with a tighter clasp?</p>
        <p>&gt; O God! can I not save</p>
        <p>&gt; One from the pitiless wave?</p>
        <p>&gt; Is all that we see or seem</p>
        <p>&gt; But a dream within a dream?</p>
        <p>Edgar Allan Poe, "A Dream Within a Dream"</p>
        <p>## What's Changed</p>
        <p>This release is about keeping LushText calm and safe under pressure. The editor stays responsive when you open large files, run big searches, or replace across a whole workspace, and your unsaved work is protected far more carefully when things go wrong.</p>
        <p>LushText now keeps its own memory use in check when you have many files open. When the combined size of loaded editor buffers grows past a comfortable limit, LushText quietly unloads the least-recently-used background tabs that are clean and reloadable, and reloads them from disk the moment you switch back. The tab you are working in is never unloaded, and any tab with unsaved changes, an untitled draft, or a document still loading or saving is always kept in memory. You may notice a background tab re-reading from disk when you return to it; you will never lose edits to it.</p>
        <p>Editing very large documents no longer freezes the window. Large buffer replacements and clears now happen in paragraph-sized slices across multiple UI turns instead of one long blocking pass, and document-sized text is coalesced and released off the main thread. This removes multi-second hangs that could previously appear when loading, replacing, or recovering huge single-line files.</p>
        <p>Searching and replacing across a workspace is more cooperative. The command palette and search now share one fuzzy-ranking path, so results are scored consistently everywhere. Search, replace preview, workspace scans, file-tree retirement, and directory traversal are all bounded so a burst of work does one aggregate pass instead of piling up. Because ranking is now unified, the order of some search results can differ from previous versions for the same query.</p>
        <p>The workspace file tree keeps up with the filesystem more reliably. When files change quickly on disk, the folder watcher restarts and refreshes responsively instead of stalling during rapid directory churn.</p>
        <p>Markdown preview handles broken local images gracefully. A corrupt or unreadable local image now shows a clear fallback block in the preview instead of stressing the rendering path.</p>
        <p>## Manual Actions Needed</p>
        <p>No manual action is needed for end users after upgrading. There is no settings reset, workspace rebuild, or app-data migration for this release. Your existing drafts, sessions, bookmarks, notes, and local history all load unchanged.</p>
        <p>Downstream packagers should build from the release commit or regenerate vendored Cargo sources from the matching `Cargo.lock`, since the Rust dependency set and Flatpak vendored sources were refreshed for this release.</p>
        <p>Maintainers and automation clients that read LushText's D-Bus automation interface should note that the read-only interface version is now 2, with additional snapshot fields for workspace-persistence progress, command-palette search and index state, and replace-preview omitted or skipped counts. Existing fields are unchanged.</p>
        <p>## Warnings and Deprecations</p>
        <p>No user-facing feature is deprecated in this release, and no on-disk format changed. Drafts, sessions, sidecar notes and bookmarks, local history, and search backups from earlier versions remain fully compatible, and downgrading to an earlier release is safe with respect to your data.</p>
        <p>When many large files are open, LushText may now unload clean background tabs to stay within its memory budget and reload them from disk on demand. This is expected behavior; it only ever affects clean, reloadable tabs, never the active tab and never unsaved work.</p>
        <p>Because fuzzy search ranking is now unified across the command palette and search, the ordering of some results may change compared with previous versions.</p>
        <p>Automatic draft recovery accepts drafts up to 64 MiB of text each; at startup LushText eagerly admits recovery bodies up to a combined 64 MiB and restores anything beyond that lazily. Very large recovery bodies are therefore restored a little later rather than all at once. Markdown preview still does not fetch remote images; only local images are rendered, and unreadable ones fall back to a clear placeholder.</p>
        <p>## Bug Fixes</p>
        <p>Fixed multi-second freezes when opening, replacing text in, or recovering very large single-line documents; large buffer edits are now sliced across UI turns instead of blocking the window.</p>
        <p>Fixed workspace search results widening the search panel or showing a horizontal scrollbar for long file paths; long paths now clip cleanly and the panel keeps a stable width.</p>
        <p>Fixed sluggish workspace file-tree refresh during rapid filesystem changes so the tree updates promptly after directories change on disk.</p>
        <p>Hardened editor crash recovery and snapshot ordering so a crash during a save is much less likely to surface stale or incomplete recovery state, and draft snapshots stay retryable rather than being silently lost.</p>
        <p>Made interrupted Replace All operations recoverable: each per-file change becomes recovery-visible before its file is replaced, and undo re-verifies the replaced bytes before restoring, closing a gap where an interrupted Replace All could leave files that could not be cleanly restored.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.5.2" date="2026-07-05">
      <description>
        <p>## Poetic Opening</p>
        <p>Source checked: Folger Shakespeare Library, "Shakespeare's Sonnets - Sonnet 53", final rhyming couplet.</p>
        <p>Selection: Complete final couplet.</p>
        <p>Complete couplet:</p>
        <p>&gt; In all external grace you have some part,</p>
        <p>&gt; But you like none, none you, for constant heart.</p>
        <p>William Shakespeare, "Sonnet 53"</p>
        <p>## What's Changed</p>
        <p>LushText now preserves editor glyphs more carefully on the active line. The editor font path was reworked so square brackets and other top-ink details stay visible with GTK 4.22 and GtkSourceView 5.20, while system fonts, custom fonts, zoom, source schemes, dark mode, and tab-content opacity continue to work together.</p>
        <p>Markdown preview is more resilient and more bounded. Local images resolve relative to the document or active workspace folders and decode away from the GTK thread, code blocks stay within a clear preview budget, and Markdown tables avoid creating runaway widget trees. Large images, very large code blocks, oversized tables, missing images, and remote images now get clear fallback blocks instead of silently stressing the UI.</p>
        <p>Workspace search is more cooperative on large result streams. Search now uses bounded backpressure, drains worker events in UI-sized batches, avoids unnecessary canonicalization for single-root searches, and keeps result-row navigation indexed so next/previous match movement stays responsive.</p>
        <p>Preferences &gt; Data has a clearer app-data format workflow. The Data page now owns its scan and conversion projection, keeps background operations off the GTK thread, shows bounded per-file details, and exposes better accessibility state for status, scan, convert, and background-opacity controls.</p>
        <p>Maintainers get a stronger release and quality gate. This release adds SonarQube Cloud analysis with quality-gate verification, enforces the 30-minute GitHub Actions job budget, bounds the release benchmark report, and hardens release recovery so benchmark tooling can be restored without moving public tags.</p>
        <p>The Rust dependency set was refreshed for the latest `anyhow` advisory fix, and Flatpak vendored sources were regenerated to match the updated lockfile.</p>
        <p>## Manual Actions Needed</p>
        <p>No manual action is needed for end users after upgrading. There is no settings reset, workspace rebuild, or app-data migration required for this release.</p>
        <p>Downstream packagers should build from the release commit or regenerate vendored Cargo sources from the matching `Cargo.lock`, because `anyhow` was updated to `1.0.103`.</p>
        <p>Maintainers who expect SonarQube Cloud analysis on private or trusted branches should keep `SONAR_TOKEN` configured. Untrusted contexts without that token will skip the analysis step by design.</p>
        <p>## Warnings and Deprecations</p>
        <p>No user-facing feature is deprecated in this release.</p>
        <p>Markdown preview still does not fetch remote images. Local preview images, code blocks, and tables now have explicit size budgets; content beyond those limits is reported inline instead of being fully rendered.</p>
        <p>Tab-content opacity can create small derived GtkSourceView style-scheme files in LushText's app data directory. If those files cannot be written, LushText falls back to the base source scheme and keeps the editor usable.</p>
        <p>## Bug Fixes</p>
        <p>Fixed active-line glyph clipping in the editor, especially visible with bracket caps in Adwaita Mono on newer GTK/GtkSourceView stacks.</p>
        <p>Fixed Markdown preview memory and responsiveness risks around large local images, oversized code blocks, and large tables.</p>
        <p>Fixed search result navigation so jumping between matches can expand and focus the indexed row directly instead of scanning the flattened result model each time.</p>
        <p>Fixed large workspace search responsiveness by bounding result delivery and processing a capped number of events per GTK tick.</p>
        <p>Fixed release benchmark publication scope so the tag-triggered benchmark report stays within the release-safe CI budget.</p>
        <p>Fixed SonarQube workflow regex and verification issues found during the first quality-gate rollout.</p>
        <p>Updated `anyhow` to `1.0.103` and refreshed Flatpak cargo sources for the associated RustSec advisory.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.5.1" date="2026-06-24">
      <description>
        <p>## Poetic Opening</p>
        <p>Source checked: Folger Shakespeare Library, "Shakespeare's Sonnets - Sonnet 60", final rhyming couplet.</p>
        <p>Selection: Complete final couplet.</p>
        <p>Complete couplet:</p>
        <p>&gt; And yet to times in hope my verse shall stand,</p>
        <p>&gt; Praising thy worth, despite his cruel hand.</p>
        <p>William Shakespeare, "Sonnet 60"</p>
        <p>## What's Changed</p>
        <p>LushText now opens Keyboard Shortcuts as a Libadwaita shortcuts dialog owned by the main window. The shortcut list itself is unchanged, but the help surface now follows the current GNOME and Libadwaita dialog model and repeated activations reuse the existing dialog instead of creating duplicate help windows.</p>
        <p>Several GTK templates received small compatibility and accessibility polish. Decorative empty-state icons are quieter for assistive technologies, the Data page's verified indicator has a clear accessible label, and generated UI templates now avoid the old accepted Blueprint warning around `GtkShortcutsWindow`.</p>
        <p>Maintainers get a stronger UI diagnostics path. This release adds a builder-diagnostics smoke lane, a reusable debug GTK runtime workflow, stricter Blueprint warning policy, refreshed template-contract evidence, and refreshed dependency and Flatpak vendoring surfaces so future UI template changes are easier to verify before they reach users.</p>
        <p>## Manual Actions Needed</p>
        <p>No manual action is needed for end users after upgrading. There is no data migration, settings reset, or workspace rebuild required for this release.</p>
        <p>Maintainers do not need to change the release process for this patch. The new builder-diagnostics runtime is a scheduled/manual diagnostics aid and does not change normal app installation.</p>
        <p>## Warnings and Deprecations</p>
        <p>No user-facing behavior is deprecated in this release.</p>
        <p>The new builder-diagnostics lane depends on a debug-enabled GTK runtime image when host GTK cannot expose builder diagnostics. If that image or GHCR access is unavailable, diagnostics may report an unsupported runtime, but LushText itself and the normal release build are unaffected.</p>
        <p>## Bug Fixes</p>
        <p>Keyboard Shortcuts no longer depends on the deprecated GTK shortcuts window template family. This fixes the help surface for newer GNOME and Libadwaita environments while keeping the existing `win.show-help-overlay` action path intact.</p>
        <p>Repeated Keyboard Shortcuts activation now focuses or presents the existing dialog owned by the window instead of risking duplicate help surfaces.</p>
        <p>Template accessibility fixes make the Open popover empty state and the Preferences data-status indicator cleaner for screen readers.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.5.0" date="2026-06-19">
      <description>
        <p>## Poetic Opening</p>
        <p>Source checked: Folger Shakespeare Library, "Sonnet 30," final couplet.</p>
        <p>Selection: Complete final couplet.</p>
        <p>Complete couplet:</p>
        <p>&gt; But if the while I think on thee, dear friend,</p>
        <p>&gt; All losses are restored and sorrows end.</p>
        <p>William Shakespeare, "Sonnet 30"</p>
        <p>## What's Changed</p>
        <p>LushText now has a much stronger GTK accessibility spine across the app. The shell, editor, command palette, Open popover, workspace sidebar, workspace search, Markdown preview, document properties, preferences, notes, bookmarks, local history, and dialogs now expose more consistent names, roles, descriptions, state, and row metadata to GTK accessibility tools.</p>
        <p>Keyboard access and assistive-technology behavior are better covered for the workspace tree and workspace header menus. The Menu key and common `Shift+F10` path now have clearer support for file, folder, workspace, and fallback actions that previously depended too much on pointer context.</p>
        <p>Search and Replace All now provide more bounded accessibility feedback around result counts, progress, preview mode, completion, cancellation, and undo availability. These announcements are intentionally capped and throttled so they help describe the workflow without dumping private document content.</p>
        <p>Accessibility validation is now a first-class maintenance surface. This release adds the accessibility guide, completion matrix, manual Orca checklist, policy check, expanded AT-SPI smoke coverage, focused smoke-case filters, assertion manifests, and visual accessibility evidence paths that make future UI work harder to regress.</p>
        <p>## Manual Actions Needed</p>
        <p>No manual action is needed for end users after upgrading. There is no data migration, settings reset, or workspace rebuild required for this release.</p>
        <p>Maintainers making release-grade accessibility claims should still pair the automated evidence with a normal GNOME Orca pass, because screen-reader speech behavior depends on the host desktop stack.</p>
        <p>## Warnings and Deprecations</p>
        <p>No user-facing behavior is deprecated in this release.</p>
        <p>Accessibility proof remains host-sensitive. AT-SPI, Orca, the compositor, theme, text scale, high-contrast mode, reduced-motion settings, and GTK behavior can vary between environments, so the new proof lanes document caveats instead of pretending every host speaks identically.</p>
        <p>Automation clients may observe additive accessibility and workspace action metadata in the action catalog, readiness surface, or snapshots. Existing actions remain on the normal GTK action paths.</p>
        <p>## Bug Fixes</p>
        <p>Workspace file and workspace-header context menus now close before their actions run, preventing stale popovers from leaking between repeated menu actions.</p>
        <p>Recycled rows in list and tree surfaces now clear and refresh accessibility metadata more deliberately, reducing stale labels, selected state, or position information after filtering, selection changes, or model replacement.</p>
        <p>Inline alerts avoid collapsing their controls into an inaccessible leaf, so recovery and response buttons stay reachable to assistive technology.</p>
        <p>The automation action catalog now includes the workspace accessibility actions that the UI exposes, keeping the automation reference and runtime catalog aligned.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.4.1" date="2026-06-18">
      <description>
        <p>## Poetic Opening</p>
        <p>Source checked: Folger Shakespeare Library, "Sonnet 65," final couplet.</p>
        <p>Selection: Complete final couplet.</p>
        <p>Complete couplet:</p>
        <p>&gt; O, none, unless this miracle have might,</p>
        <p>&gt; That in black ink my love may still shine bright.</p>
        <p>William Shakespeare, "Sonnet 65"</p>
        <p>## What's Changed</p>
        <p>LushText now tightens minimap projection behavior so viewport, content-row anchors, and markers follow GtkSourceMap's rendered widget coordinates more directly. The result is a steadier minimap around document edges, dense layouts, and visual proof checks.</p>
        <p>Replace All now scans line endings with a faster byte-search path and has clearer behavior at its documented file-size and undo-journal limits. The intended editing result stays the same, while leading newlines, CRLF text, and files exactly at safety caps are covered more carefully.</p>
        <p>Startup and persistence recovery paths are more conservative around drafts, metadata quarantine, Replace All undo journals, local history reconciliation, sidecar moves, and format-upgrade backups. When evidence is ambiguous or damaged, LushText is better covered to preserve it and report diagnostics instead of cleaning it up too eagerly.</p>
        <p>Developers get safer local desktop staging. `make run` and `scripts/run-dev-app.sh` stage the development launcher under `dev.cominotti.lushtext.Devel`, quarantine stale production-ID desktop shadows, and keep the installed Flatpak launcher visible.</p>
        <p>Maintainers also get stronger mutation-testing controls, including focused mutant regex and exclude options plus broader coverage for filesystem, recovery, minimap, search, and persistence edge cases.</p>
        <p>## Manual Actions Needed</p>
        <p>No manual action is needed for normal users upgrading from v0.4.0.</p>
        <p>Developers who previously staged local desktop entries and still see stale launcher behavior can run `make clear-lushtext-xdg` once. This is not needed for ordinary Flatpak upgrades.</p>
        <p>## Warnings and Deprecations</p>
        <p>There are no deprecations in this release.</p>
        <p>This release adds `memchr` as a direct dependency for Replace All line scanning. Downstream packagers should build from the release commit or regenerate vendored Cargo sources from the matching `Cargo.lock`.</p>
        <p>## Bug Fixes</p>
        <p>Fixed minimap projection edge cases around line-bottom calculation, reversed projected bounds, native slider fitting, and non-finite geometry inputs.</p>
        <p>Fixed development desktop staging that could leave a local production-ID desktop file shadowing the installed Flatpak launcher.</p>
        <p>Hardened exact-limit behavior for Replace All, draft preload, recovery metadata, and Replace All undo journals so values at the documented caps remain accepted while over-limit data stays guarded.</p>
        <p>Improved recovery coverage for drafts, local history, sidecar metadata, migration ledgers, and format-upgrade backups so damaged or ambiguous user-data evidence is preserved more predictably.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.4.0" date="2026-06-16">
      <description>
        <p>## Poetic Opening</p>
        <p>Source checked: Folger Shakespeare Library, "Sonnet 18," final couplet.</p>
        <p>Selection: Complete final couplet.</p>
        <p>Complete stanza:</p>
        <p>&gt; So long as men can breathe or eyes can see,</p>
        <p>&gt; So long lives this, and this gives life to thee.</p>
        <p>William Shakespeare, "Sonnet 18"</p>
        <p>## What's Changed</p>
        <p>LushText now has a GNOME-style Open popover for recent documents. The header Open button and Ctrl+K open a searchable recent list with file names, parent paths, age labels, row removal, empty/search states, and a direct path back to the file chooser. Recent rows are app-owned and bounded: LushText records local file paths and timestamps after explicit opens, excludes files that are already open, prunes missing or unsupported entries, and keeps startup session restore from reshuffling your recent history.</p>
        <p>The command palette can now search note records directly. Notes mode searches bookmarks, folder notes, document notes, and note-related open tabs using the same note taxonomy as Browse Notes, including previews and workspace-aware grouping. All mode now includes notes alongside files and commands, so remembered context is reachable without opening a separate notes browser first.</p>
        <p>Note editing feels calmer. Existing notes open in rendered preview mode when there is meaningful saved content, empty notes open in edit mode, and Save is available only for meaningful changes instead of whitespace-only edits.</p>
        <p>The tab strip now stays visible even when only one normal tab is open, making tab context actions easier to discover and keeping the document chrome more consistent.</p>
        <p>Preferences and supporting tools received polish too. The Background Opacity wording now more clearly describes editor and Markdown preview document backgrounds, the Data page wording is tidier, and the development surface gained focused command-palette-notes smoke/manual fixtures plus stronger visual proof coverage for the new Open popover.</p>
        <p>The Snap packaging work is now captured in OpenSpec and maintainer documentation as preparation work. This release does not add a new public Snap channel.</p>
        <p>## Manual Actions Needed</p>
        <p>No manual action is needed for normal users upgrading from v0.3.0.</p>
        <p>Maintainers should review the release workflow output after publication: the Cominotti Flatpak repository remains the primary Flatpak channel, while any Flathub handoff is optional and depends on the configured GitHub Actions secrets. Snap packaging remains preparation-only until its external store and platform gates are ready.</p>
        <p>## Warnings and Deprecations</p>
        <p>LushText now writes an app-owned `recent-documents.json` file for the Open popover. It stores local paths, canonical identities when available, and last-opened timestamps, but not document text, note bodies, drafts, local-history contents, or private sidecar identifiers.</p>
        <p>Recent Open currently supports local filesystem documents only. Unsupported URI-style entries are ignored or pruned instead of being offered as reopen targets.</p>
        <p>Malformed or unreadable note, bookmark, or sidecar metadata may be skipped from note search while LushText records recovery diagnostics. This keeps search safe and bounded, but it means damaged metadata may not appear in the command palette until the underlying sidecar is recoverable.</p>
        <p>No deprecations are introduced in this release.</p>
        <p>## Bug Fixes</p>
        <p>Recent Open now refreshes from the real open-tab set, so stale open-path cache entries no longer hide valid recent documents after tabs close, rename, save-as, or external-file transitions.</p>
        <p>The Open popover rows better match the intended GNOME-style shape: long paths use tooltips and ellipsizing instead of widening the popover, the list avoids horizontal scrolling, and search/reset behavior is steadier across repeated openings.</p>
        <p>Command palette note refreshes are non-destructive, so loading or updating note rows no longer clears the active palette query or disrupts the visible search state.</p>
        <p>Folder-note wording in AppStream metadata now matches the current user-facing model, and the Background Opacity preference text is clearer about what becomes transparent.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.3.0" date="2026-06-14">
      <description>
        <p>## Poetic Opening</p>
        <p>Source checked: Wikisource, "The Poems of Oscar Wilde/Symphony in Yellow" from The Poems of Oscar Wilde (1909), first printed stanza.</p>
        <p>Selection: Complete first stanza.</p>
        <p>Complete stanza:</p>
        <p>&gt; An omnibus across the bridge</p>
        <p>&gt; Crawls like a yellow butterfly,</p>
        <p>&gt; And, here and there, a passer-by</p>
        <p>&gt; Shows like a little restless midge.</p>
        <p>Oscar Wilde, "Symphony in Yellow"</p>
        <p>## What's Changed</p>
        <p>LushText workspaces are now named, ordered folder sets instead of one-folder roots. You can create an empty workspace by name, add one or more folders afterward, reorder top-level folders without moving anything on disk, collapse busy workspace sections, and keep overlapping folders visible in the sidebar while search, the command palette, notes, and bookmarks de-duplicate results by file identity.</p>
        <p>The app-data safety story is much stronger. LushText now has a startup format preflight, a `Preferences &gt; Data` status page, backup-before-replacement behavior for future format upgrades or Start Fresh decisions, recovery quarantine for damaged metadata, retryable migration ledgers after in-app renames, and clearer diagnostics when session, draft, sidecar, local-history, search, or Replace All evidence cannot be fully trusted.</p>
        <p>Saving and Replace All now share more of the same durable filesystem contract. Writes coordinate by stable target, preserve symlink-backed saves by updating the resolved file, distinguish ordinary write failures from durability-unconfirmed writes, and keep Replace All undo evidence in per-file durable journals with safety caps for large or actively changing files.</p>
        <p>The workspace sidebar is easier to read while you work. Sticky click selection no longer pretends that a row is special, open files and the active tab now have explicit row indicators, reorder handles refresh after sidebar mutations, zero-folder workspaces have clearer empty states, and the workspace sidebar plus document properties pane now share a calmer side-rail style.</p>
        <p>Several everyday editor surfaces received polish: the bottom status bar is more legible without becoming a second toolbar, the minimap behaves more steadily during sidebar animation and dense viewport changes, inline alerts sit more naturally in the editor, and Markdown preview now handles reference-style and markdown-it-style inline footnotes more faithfully.</p>
        <p>Maintainers and automation users get a larger proof surface. LushText now exposes a documented read-only D-Bus automation snapshot, a machine-readable action catalog, reusable automation client commands, visual/crash/portal/accessibility/performance smoke lanes, GTK Lush proof tooling, Blueprint template checks, mutation testing, and release-lane fixes for Flatpak and Flathub handoff workflows.</p>
        <p>## Manual Actions Needed</p>
        <p>No manual action is needed for normal users upgrading from v0.2.0.</p>
        <p>If you are building LushText from source, update to Rust 1.96.0 or newer and install `blueprint-compiler` before regenerating UI templates. Maintainers should still confirm whether the release workflow deploys the Cominotti Flatpak repository directly or uploads the `cominotti-flatpak-repository` artifact for manual publication, and should review any optional Flathub pull request before merging it.</p>
        <p>## Warnings and Deprecations</p>
        <p>Folder notes now use consistent folder-note terminology. Existing legacy sidecars remain recognized as compatibility data, but new user-facing labels and current storage use current folder-note terms.</p>
        <p>Public-era v1 app metadata is expected to continue normally. Older pre-public or unsupported bare JSON metadata is not silently treated as current data; LushText preserves it through recovery or format-upgrade backup paths before starting fresh or writing a current replacement.</p>
        <p>Future-version app metadata written by a newer LushText cannot be downgraded by this release. If that state is detected at startup, the safest choice is to quit and reopen it with the newer app; Start Fresh is available only after preservation succeeds.</p>
        <p>The minimap and Replace All safety limits remain intentionally conservative for very large or rapidly changing files. LushText may hide expensive minimap details, skip files that changed after search, or refuse undo payloads that exceed configured safety bounds to protect responsiveness and user data.</p>
        <p>Snap packaging remains preparation-only until its external store and GNOME platform gates are ready. The Flatpak continues to use full filesystem access for local workspace editing and does not move to portals-only file access in this release.</p>
        <p>## Bug Fixes</p>
        <p>Workspace folder reorder handles now resync after folder add, remove, reorder, refresh, and row-recycling transitions, so drag affordances do not go stale while the sidebar changes around them.</p>
        <p>Minimap viewport diagnostics, marker placement, overscroll handling, and sidebar animation interactions are steadier, reducing visual drift and layout surprises in dense documents and constrained windows.</p>
        <p>Replace All undo recovery is more robust after partial failures, and modified or actively saving tabs are skipped more consistently instead of being overwritten by a broad replace operation.</p>
        <p>Recovery metadata handling now preserves malformed, oversized, unreadable, unsupported, or unsafe app-owned state before defaults or repairs are written, reducing the chance that a startup recovery problem becomes silent data loss.</p>
        <p>The release lane includes fixes for the Cominotti Flatpak workflow, Flathub runtime remote setup, trusted workspace handling in container jobs, and recovery-ref reruns when publication tooling needs to be repaired after a public tag exists.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.2.0" date="2026-06-01">
      <description>
        <p>## Poetic Opening</p>
        <p>Source checked: Folger Shakespeare Library, "Shakespeare's Sonnets - Sonnet 116", final rhyming couplet.</p>
        <p>Selection: Complete final couplet.</p>
        <p>Complete couplet:</p>
        <p>&gt; If this be error, and upon me proved,</p>
        <p>&gt; I never writ, nor no man ever loved.</p>
        <p>William Shakespeare, "Sonnet 116"</p>
        <p>## What's Changed</p>
        <p>LushText now has a first-party Cominotti Flatpak publication path. The release workflow can generate signed repository metadata for the Cominotti Flatpak remote, publish the shared `cominotti.flatpakrepo` remote descriptor, and provide a LushText-specific `lushtext.flatpakref` installer while keeping Flathub as a reviewable secondary handoff.</p>
        <p>The editor minimap is more faithful to the document you are actually seeing. Search, bookmark, modified-line, and long-line markers now project through the rendered GtkSourceMap geometry, so they stay aligned with wrapped text and dynamic end-of-file overscroll instead of drifting into the blank tail below the document.</p>
        <p>Adaptive editor geometry is steadier in tight windows. The status bar and its quick document controls stay visible, the workspace sidebar and document properties settle into pane or sheet presentations without oscillating, and passive window narrowing no longer leaves the editor's left edge hidden behind the workspace surface.</p>
        <p>Changing editor transparency and theme settings is smoother across multiple tabs. LushText now deduplicates generated opacity-aware GtkSourceView style schemes and writes them off the main thread, keeping preference changes from competing with editing responsiveness.</p>
        <p>Maintainers also get a stronger release lane: Cominotti Flatpak verification, Cloudflare Pages limit checks, optional deploy hooks, Flathub source-commit correctness, release dry-run coverage, and headless Mutter debugging helpers for reproducing GTK layout issues.</p>
        <p>## Manual Actions Needed</p>
        <p>No manual action is needed for normal users upgrading from v0.1.0.</p>
        <p>Maintainers should verify that the release workflow either deploys the Cominotti Flatpak repository or uploads the `cominotti-flatpak-repository` artifact for manual publication. Optional Flathub publication still requires review of the generated pull request or artifact; automerge remains intentionally disabled.</p>
        <p>## Warnings and Deprecations</p>
        <p>There are no deprecations in this release.</p>
        <p>Normal editor windows now keep a slightly taller minimum height so the header, tab strip, editor, and status bar remain usable instead of clipping persistent controls.</p>
        <p>The minimap may hide itself for very large wrapped documents with extremely long lines. This is intentional: it protects responsiveness when a narrow minimap would otherwise need to build an enormous wrapped visual-line map.</p>
        <p>Cominotti Flatpak deployment depends on the configured signing material and deployment credentials. If those secrets or variables are absent, the release workflow reports the skipped publication step instead of claiming completion.</p>
        <p>## Bug Fixes</p>
        <p>Minimap markers now stay inside the rendered document content area and no longer stretch across blank end-of-file overscroll space.</p>
        <p>The workspace sidebar, document-properties pane, compact bottom sheet, search results, and status bar now share constrained window space more predictably, reducing GTK allocation warnings and preventing important controls from disappearing at small sizes.</p>
        <p>Window resize transitions now preserve the editor's left and top edges when the user was already at the start of the document, avoiding accidental-looking horizontal or vertical scroll jumps caused by layout changes.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.1.0" date="2026-05-29">
      <description>
        <p>## Poetic Opening</p>
        <p>Source checked: Folger Shakespeare Library, "Shakespeare's Sonnets - Sonnet 18", final rhyming couplet.</p>
        <p>Selection: Complete final couplet.</p>
        <p>Complete couplet:</p>
        <p>&gt; So long as men can breathe, or eyes can see,</p>
        <p>&gt; So long lives this, and this gives life to thee.</p>
        <p>William Shakespeare, "Sonnet 18"</p>
        <p>## What's Changed</p>
        <p>LushText's first public release is here: a fast, native GNOME text editor built with GTK4, Libadwaita, GtkSourceView, and a workspace-first shape. You can open files directly, keep multiple tabs restored across launches, pin useful tabs, save and print documents, and tune the editor with dark-mode-aware syntax schemes, custom fonts, line numbers, wrapping, zoom, and GSettings-backed preferences.</p>
        <p>Workspace support is the heart of this release. You can add named workspace folders, switch between a single workspace and `All workspaces`, browse files in the persistent sidebar, inspect files with quick peek, drill into deep folders with Focus Folder, and let visible folders refresh as the filesystem changes.</p>
        <p>Editing is paired with recovery and safety work. LushText autosaves drafts, restores sessions, detects external file changes, uses durable atomic saves, offers explicit save/discard close flows, preserves line-ending and encoding state, and degrades intentionally on very large files instead of freezing or risking data loss.</p>
        <p>Search and navigation cover both the current tab and the current workspace scope. The command palette searches files and commands, per-tab find/replace handles local edits, and workspace search supports streaming results, regex or literal modes, whole-word matching, glob filters, search history, saved searches, match navigation, and a previewable multi-file Replace All flow with undo.</p>
        <p>Markdown files get a native preview alongside the source editor or as a focused full-width view. The renderer covers headings, emphasis, code, links, ordered and unordered lists, task lists, nested blockquotes, GitHub alert callouts, footnotes, definition lists, tables, and local images with clear fallback states.</p>
        <p>LushText also ships line bookmarks, document notes, folder notes, a unified notes browser, local history snapshots with preview and restore undo, a minimap with useful document markers, adaptive document properties, and Focus Mode for a quieter writing surface.</p>
        <p>## Manual Actions Needed</p>
        <p>No manual action is needed for this first release.</p>
        <p>Maintainers publishing through Flathub should still review the generated Flathub pull request before merging. The workflow intentionally does not enable Flathub automerge.</p>
        <p>## Warnings and Deprecations</p>
        <p>There are no upgrade deprecations because this is the first public GitHub release.</p>
        <p>Large files intentionally lose some editor features as they grow: files above 10 MB disable syntax highlighting, files above 50 MB disable undo and local-history browsing, and files above 500 MB are refused.</p>
        <p>The Flatpak uses broad host filesystem access because workspace editing, search, rename, delete, file monitoring, notes, and local history all need normal local-file behavior. LushText does not request network access.</p>
        <p>Bookmark, note, and local-history sidecars follow saved file paths and in-app renames, but they do not automatically follow external moves or copies performed outside LushText.</p>
        <p>EditorConfig support currently covers indentation settings only. Line endings, charset, trailing-whitespace trimming, final-newline insertion, and max-line-length handling are not applied yet.</p>
        <p>Snap packaging is scaffolded, but it is not ready for users until the GNOME 50 Snap platform is available.</p>
        <p>## Bug Fixes</p>
        <p>This first release includes the reliability fixes that made the public cut: durable save ordering, draft/session recovery hardening, safer close flows, and preservation of modified state when a save fails.</p>
        <p>It also includes polish and correctness fixes across the workspace sidebar, search panel, Markdown preview layout, inline alerts, desktop identity, Flatpak packaging, and headless widget-test harness.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
  </releases>

  <translation type="gettext">lushtext</translation>

